.TH CLRUCACHE_GET_FULL 3 "2026-08-02" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
clrucache_get_full \- get a value by key
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/clrucache.h>

ccol_retval_t clrucache_get_full(clru_cache cache,
                                 const cmap_pair *key_pair,
                                 cmap_pair *val_out);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Looks up key_pair in the cache. On success it allocates a copy of the
paired value on the heap. It uses the custom allocator of the cache if
the caller gave one at construction, and malloc() if not. It assigns
that pointer to val_out->ptr, and it sets val_out->size to the stored
byte size. The caller must free val_out->ptr with the matching
allocator. That is the custom free function if the caller configured a
custom allocator, and free() if not.

Cache hit: the library moves the entry to the most recently used
position. It writes a copy of the value of that entry, on the heap, into
val_out.

Cache miss and no remote getter: the function returns
ccol_key_not_found.

Cache miss with a remote getter: the library inserts a placeholder
entry. It then calls the remote getter outside the lock of the segment.
Concurrent get calls for the same key coalesce. Only the first caller
runs the fetch. All the other callers block until the result is ready,
and they get the same result as the caller that ran the fetch. When the
getter succeeds, the library caches the fetched value and writes a copy
of it, on the heap, into val_out for every waiter. When the getter fails
in an ordinary way, every waiter gets ccol_key_not_found. The library
can also reject the fetched value because it has the wrong size for the
own fixed-size buffer of a concurrent, coalesced clru_get() caller.
Every waiter then gets ccol_unexpected_failure, and this call gets it
too when it is one of those waiters.

This call blocks while a set for the same key is in progress. It waits
until the set completes and the cache is in a consistent state. When
the set fails and the key is then absent from the cache, this call
continues exactly as a get that starts after the failed set: it runs
the remote getter (or joins a fetch of the same key that has already
started), and it reports ccol_key_not_found only when the cache has no
remote getter.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
cache     Cache handle.
key_pair  The key to look up. The ptr and size fields must be set.
val_out   The output pair that the function fills on success.
          val_out->ptr becomes a copy of the value on the heap. The
          caller must free it with the custom allocator of the cache,
          or with free() when the caller gave no custom allocator.
          val_out->size becomes the size of the stored value. val_out
          must not be NULL.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success           The function filled val_out. The caller owns
                       val_out->ptr.
ccol_key_not_found     The key is absent and there is no getter, or
                       the getter failed.
ccol_invalid_args      cache is CLRU_CACHE_INVALID, or cache is a
                       stale handle that a call already destroyed, or
                       key_pair is NULL, or val_out is NULL, or
                       key_pair->size does not agree with the width of
                       the key type of the cache.
ccol_not_enough_memory An allocation failed. It is the allocation of
                       the placeholder entry or of the output copy.
ccol_unexpected_failure
                       This call coalesced onto another, concurrent
                       cache-miss fetch for the same key. The own
                       caller of that fetch rejected the fetched value,
                       because the value has the wrong size for its own
                       fixed-size destination buffer. Every caller that
                       coalesces onto the same fetch gets this same
                       result.
ccol_container_full    The internal bookkeeping of the cache reached
                       its maximum element count on a cache miss. A
                       real program cannot reach this.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
cache is an opaque VALUE handle. The library resolves it through a slot
table that the library owns, before it touches the cache object behind
the handle. That resolve takes a pin, and this call holds the pin for
its whole duration. This includes any time that the call blocks inside
a remote getter callback. A concurrent clru_destroy() on the same handle
therefore waits for this call to finish. It does not free the cache
under this call.
The val_out->ptr that the function gives back comes from the custom
allocator of the cache, if the caller gave one at construction. It comes
from the standard malloc() if not. Free it with the matching allocator.
When the value type is char*, the copy is a null-terminated string on
the heap. This allocation also comes from the custom allocator, or from
malloc() when the caller configured none.
The cache never gives out a pointer into its internal storage. Such a
pointer dangles after an eviction.
The average-case complexity of the hash map lookup is O(1).
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR clru_get (3),
.BR clrucache_set_full (3),
.BR clrucache_create_full (3)
